<title>GERMANY: Hoechst sells Henning Berlin to French firm.</title>
<headline>Hoechst sells Henning Berlin to French firm.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1996-09-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Germany's Hoechst AG said on Friday that it has sold its Henning Berlin GmbH subsidary to French-based Synthelabo.</p>
<p>Terms of the deal were not disclosed.</p>
<p>Hoechst said it had received Henning Berlin, a speciality supplier of thyroid medications, when it acquired Marion Merrell Dow in 1995.</p>
<p>Hoechst said the sale of Henning, which had 1995 revenues of 80 million marks ($52.8 million) , was part of the group's decision to divest non-core operations.</p>
<p>Synthelabo is a subisdiary of French cosmetics group L'Oreal . Synthelabo had revenues in 1995 of about 210 million marks in Germany and will take over the 239 workers at Henning.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525 ($ = 1.516 German Marks)</p>
</text>
